00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.440
What are event-driven architectures? And how can&nbsp;
they enable real-time user interactions, pluggable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:14.160
microservices, and extensible streaming and data&nbsp;
analytics? My name is Whitney, I'm on the Cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:21.600
team here at IBM. Before I dig in on event driven&nbsp;
architecture, specifically I'd like to talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:29.360
system design, more generally and specifically&nbsp;
the reactive manifesto. The reactive manifesto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:35.920
is a set of community-driven guidelines that are&nbsp;
intended to give a cohesive approach to systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:43.840
design. So, at the core of the reactant manifesto&nbsp;
we have, we want our system to be message driven.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.240
And so, specifically asynchronous messaging.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:00.400
And then also, we want our our&nbsp;
system design to be scalable,

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.000
and we want it to be resilient.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:18.560
So this implies distributed systems or Kubernetes.&nbsp;
So by scalable we mean that we want the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:23.760
hardware used to expand as the workload&nbsp;
expands and contract as the workload contracts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.000
and by resilient we mean we we don't&nbsp;
want any single point of failure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.480
and if the system does fail we want it to be able&nbsp;
to tolerate tolerate that elegantly so with this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:40.960
foundation in place we should be able to build a&nbsp;
system that is responsive responsive is the value

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.040
on top of that we can expect&nbsp;
a system that is maintainable

00:01:53.520 --> 00:02:02.080
so it means it's easy to change easy to fix a bug&nbsp;
or do an update and we want one that is extensible

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.840
so we should be able to expand the system easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:13.440
so now that we have the core of what we&nbsp;
want our systems designed to look like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.400
now let's talk about let's level set&nbsp;
here and talk about like what is an event&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:26.720
so an event is a statement of fact an immutable&nbsp;
statement of fact about something that happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:32.160
in the past so let's give that some context&nbsp;
we'll talk about a retail application&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.360
so a retail application has a checkout service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:43.520
and that checkout service is going to want&nbsp;
to communicate with an inventory service

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:52.320
a shipping service and a contact service

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:03.840
so with the messaging model if the inventory wants&nbsp;
to know what the checkout is doing the checkout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.080
will send a message directly to inventory&nbsp;
to let the inventory know a checkout happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:14.240
and another one directly to shipping and directly&nbsp;
to contact so with messaging we have targeted

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:23.120
delivery um not only that but inventory&nbsp;
can maybe send a message back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:31.840
checkout it can be a conversational&nbsp;
delivery conversational messaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:38.720
and then finally let's talk about where that&nbsp;
message lives before it's sent it's it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:44.560
lives on the host service or on the producing&nbsp;
service when it's received it lives on the um&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:50.880
it lives on the host service until it's received&nbsp;
by the consuming service or in the case of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:56.960
message broker it might sit on a machine in&nbsp;
between but regardless the the data persistence is

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:59.840
transient

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:10.160
now let's compare that to eventing so if&nbsp;
our checkout service is producing events&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:14.960
it's producing an event every time a checkout&nbsp;
happens without any concern to which service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:21.040
is consuming that and so with that we have a&nbsp;
system that's highly scalable so we have um&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:28.400
three services here interested in the checkout&nbsp;
but even if we expand it to a hundred services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:33.280
listening to check out that wouldn't affect&nbsp;
what the checkout service is doing in any way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:39.280
not only that when we have all the checkouts&nbsp;
in a row we come up with an event log&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:42.720
and it can be replayable&nbsp;
so we have a stream history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:46.720
and a history of everything that that&nbsp;
every checkout event that has happened

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:56.640
and then our data persistence depending on how the&nbsp;
cluster is set up it can be set up to be immutable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:01.920
it can be there forever as long as you have the&nbsp;
physical hardware to back it up so we have um&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:06.960
instead of having transient data persistent&nbsp;
so we have uh permanent or possibly permanent

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:22.720
not immutable but permanent data persistence e and&nbsp;
t um so this is the foundation of our event driven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:29.360
architecture so we have a resilient a reactive&nbsp;
responsive system design and then that message&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:38.640
driven element is is event messaging specifically&nbsp;
so when we go into our proper event design the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:44.960
first thing we're going to consider are our event&nbsp;
producers so we might have a web application&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:52.400
that produces events and we&nbsp;
might have a a mobile application

00:05:55.120 --> 00:06:00.240
and let's say we also have an edge device that's&nbsp;
producing events so uh internet of things device

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:06.400
and all of these are producing&nbsp;
events into our system so

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:14.880
so this piece of the system here that&nbsp;
has all the events running through it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:19.840
this is called the event backbone

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:28.240
so what does this system enable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:34.400
well first of all we have our event logs being&nbsp;
produced by all the producing applications

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:46.640
so the event logs can do a few things first it&nbsp;
can trigger an action so if we have an internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:52.080
of things device let's say our internet of things&nbsp;
device is a robot vacuum cleaner and when we turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:07:00.720
on that robot vacuum cleaner it spins up a pod&nbsp;
on the infrastructure and that pod is a function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:06.400
as a service so function as a service is a modular&nbsp;
piece of code often associated with an edge device&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:13.680
that doesn't exist on a server on the server until&nbsp;
it is turned on and that's when it is spun up so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:18.880
that's called serverless function as a service&nbsp;
is sits on top of serverless infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:28.640
but the point is that event triggers an action&nbsp;
of the with the robot vacuum cleaner to spin up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:34.160
bounce all around the house make everything&nbsp;
squeaky clean and then when the function is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:39.520
finished running it powers down and perhaps&nbsp;
that'll cause our function as a service to send&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:44.480
another event back into the system to let&nbsp;
it know that the the function is complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:52.480
another cool thing about the event log is it&nbsp;
can be used to help optimize and customize data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:59.120
persistence so if our web application is our maybe&nbsp;
it's our retail application that we talked about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:06.560
before and it's doing a stream of checkout events&nbsp;
well our inventory service can be reading from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:13.200
that and it's keeping track of a local data&nbsp;
store that's keeping track of the inventory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:22.800
so our inventory service will consume from&nbsp;
that data stream from the web application it'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:29.040
modify the local data and then it'll produce&nbsp;
again into the event backbone and so this new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:34.000
stream is giving the most current inventory&nbsp;
to any other application in the system that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:40.240
wants to consume from it and so while we're&nbsp;
talking about data another thing that happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:46.960
we can save all of the data that comes through the&nbsp;
system so all the raw data or all of the transform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:53.840
data here at the end once it goes through the&nbsp;
system we'll save it all in a huge data lake

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:00.640
and this is what's going to power&nbsp;
really data-heavy applications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:06.160
like ai for example and the data lake is&nbsp;
accessible from anywhere in the system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:09.120
that any service that you let consume from it

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:21.840
so another thing that our event-driven&nbsp;
architectures can enable are a stream processing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:28.000
so this is built on top of&nbsp;
the apache kafka streams api&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:37.040
so let's say that our mobile app is producing&nbsp;
let's say it's a music streaming service and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:42.160
every time let's say up until your in your whole&nbsp;
life up until this point you've been listening to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:48.000
only miley cyrus and britney spears but today you&nbsp;
decide you know what i want to see what jay-z is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:54.160
about and you turn jay-z on well immediately&nbsp;
your streaming app is showing you kanye west&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.160 --> 00:10:01.040
and lauren hill and giving you ideas about what&nbsp;
you might want to listen to next so that stream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:07.840
processing is a really powerful and what gives&nbsp;
the gives real-time calculations like if it's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:16.240
ride share app you um that you know exactly how&nbsp;
far away your ride is but it can also be used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:21.600
implement business rules and business policies&nbsp;
and it can be used for real-time analytics

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:31.840
and then let's also talk about with&nbsp;
our system the communications layer

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:40.480
so specifically it's called pub sub communication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:49.920
for publish subscribe so we could have any&nbsp;
microservice a containerized microservice

00:10:54.080 --> 00:11:01.360
and just like we talked about before with it being&nbsp;
extensible we can very easily plug it into we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:07.440
write a new microservice and plug it into our&nbsp;
system and immediately have it start consuming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:13.120
from any stream and producing to a new stream or&nbsp;
even producing to a stream that already exists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:17.120
and none of the other applications need&nbsp;
to be concerned with it at all so it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

